course: dictionary
lesson: 12961
title: Strong words, strong feelings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word excruciating means extremely painful. «The broken bone gave Sam excruciating pain, so he called for help at once.» The pain is real and very strong, so the best thing to do is to get help quickly.
explain: People also use excruciating in a joking, exaggerated way. «Tom's poem in assembly was excruciating embarrassment.» Tom did not feel physical pain. The word shows that the embarrassment was almost too much to bear. Check the clues: is anyone hurt? If not, it is probably the joking meaning.
explain: Ask the same question as always: what would fit here? Swap in very painful or terribly awkward and see if the sentence still makes sense. If someone tells you a pain is excruciating, take it seriously and tell an adult straight away.
fact: Excruciating means extremely painful.
fact: In everyday talk, excruciating can also mean terribly embarrassing, boring or awkward.
fact: When a real pain is excruciating, it is a reason to get help quickly.
fact: «Excruciate» is a rare verb that means to cause great pain.
fact: Real pain and joking exaggeration are different: check whether anyone is actually hurt.
exercise: «The broken bone gave Sam excruciating pain.» What does excruciating mean here? | Very funny | Slightly itchy | *Extremely painful | Quiet and calm
exercise: Maya says the school play was excruciating, because she was bored and cringing the whole time. What does excruciating mean in her sentence? | Very cold | *Terribly embarrassing, boring or awkward | Extremely painful | Full of light
exercise: «Her toothache was so excruciating that she could not sleep.» Find and copy the word that means extremely painful. | toothache | could | *excruciating | sleep
test: What does an excruciating pain tell you about what to do? | It means the pain is funny | *It is a reason to get help quickly | It is a reason to ignore it until it goes | It means you should go to sleep
test: «The maths lesson felt excruciating, though nobody was hurt.» What does excruciating mean here? | Very cold | Extremely painful, like a broken leg | Bright and glowing | *Terribly boring or awkward, not a real injury
exam: «The cut on his knee was excruciating, so Ella called her mum straight away.» What does excruciating tell you? | *It is a real, very strong pain that needs help quickly | Only a little sore | Nothing at all | It is a funny joke
